00:50:26,766 --> 00:50:30,052
And we're going to make a fetch request to this URL we just copied.

531
00:50:30,638 --> 00:50:38,688
We can even store that URL above so it's a bit easier to read. So say const pokey API

532
00:50:39,568 --> 00:50:42,820
URL is equal to this URL right here.

533
00:50:43,854 --> 00:50:47,332
And now we can just make a fetch to this Poke API URL.

534
00:50:47,662 --> 00:50:49,828
But let's not go with this single Pokemon.

535
00:50:50,032 --> 00:50:51,396
Let's fetch all of them.

536
00:50:51,726 --> 00:51:00,032
Then once we make a request, we can actually parse the response by saying data is equal to awaitresponse.json.

537
00:51:00,782 --> 00:51:05,472
Then we can add a console log saying loader data.

538
00:51:05,632 --> 00:51:10,832
And then we can actually consolog the data and return that data right here.

539
00:51:11,022 --> 00:51:23,392
So now to be able to access the data from the loader, the only thing you have to do is go within your component or route and say cons data is equal to route.useloader

540
00:51:23,632 --> 00:51:24,032
data.

541
00:51:24,462 --> 00:51:25,072
And that's it.

542
00:51:25,358 --> 00:51:28,644
If you've done that, you can just very simply use that data.

543
00:51:29,038 --> 00:51:39,712
So I'll comment out these cards and right at the top, below the h1, I'll render a UL with a class name set to margin top of six,

544
00:51:40,046 --> 00:51:44,912
a list set to none, padding of zero, and space dash y-5.

545
00:51:45,774 --> 00:51:54,116
And then within it, I'll map over the data by saying data.results dot map, where we get each individual Pokemon.

