1708
02:36:24,334 --> 02:36:28,640
Today, Firebase offers three different database engines.

1709
02:36:28,800 --> 02:36:34,880
And understanding why we aren't using one over the other is what makes you a senior developer.

1710
02:36:35,040 --> 02:36:40,644
The first type is a real-time database, a giant single JSON tree.

1711
02:36:41,038 --> 02:36:48,356
It's ultra fast for simple state syncing, like a typing indicator, but you can do complex querying.

1712
02:36:48,718 --> 02:36:57,956
So if you want to find skills created by users with a specific email address, you have to download almost everything and then filter it out yourself.

1713
02:36:58,160 --> 02:37:06,240
Then there's the Cloud Firestore, a no SQL document store, which means that you're dealing with collections and documents,

1714
02:37:06,478 --> 02:37:10,564
similar to MongoDB, and it scales to millions of users.

1715
02:37:11,520 --> 02:37:14,884
But there are no joins support.

1716
02:37:15,310 --> 02:37:24,868
So if a skill needs to show a user's email, you either duplicate the email, risking stale data, or make two separate network calls.

1717
02:37:25,486 --> 02:37:28,708
And then there's Firebase Data Connect.

1718
02:37:28,814 --> 02:37:34,308
It uses Cloud SQL based on Postgres and it allows for relational mapping.

1719
02:37:34,606 --> 02:37:40,484
So you can define a user once and a skill once and then join them in a single query.

1720
02:37:40,720 --> 02:37:44,900
And more importantly, you don't have to write SQL strings manually.

1721
02:37:45,390 --> 02:37:46,564
You use GraphQL.

1722
02:37:47,022 --> 02:37:55,972
Firebase then generates a type safe SDK specifically for your app so your TypeScript code knows exactly what data is coming back.

1723
02:37:56,334 --> 02:38:00,132
And this works super seamlessly with Tanstack Start applications.

1724
02:38:00,494 --> 02:38:04,356
So that's why we'll be using Firebase Data Connect within this course.

