What does that even mean?

1763
02:40:37,518 --> 02:40:41,668
Well, it's all about thinking in terms of entities and relationships.

1764
02:40:42,062 --> 02:40:45,796
So first, identify the entities of the application you're developing.

1765
02:40:46,190 --> 02:40:51,044
In this case, the first entity is gonna be the person who creates skills.

1766
02:40:51,598 --> 02:40:58,400
They have an identity, which is the clerk ID, and profile attributes such as the profile image, email, and name.

1767
02:40:58,542 --> 02:41:04,612
And then the second entity is gonna be the actual skill, which is the core asset of the application.

1768
02:41:05,070 --> 02:41:09,156
It has a title, logic, such as the prompt and the usage,

1769
02:41:09,870 --> 02:41:12,320
and some additional metadata, such as the tags.

1770
02:41:12,494 --> 02:41:25,200
Then we need to define the relationships between the entities, such as one-to-many, like one user can create many different skills, and one skill can only belong to exactly one user.

1771
02:41:25,360 --> 02:41:29,604
In dataconnect, you don't nest the user inside the skill.

1772
02:41:30,160 --> 02:41:32,100
You create a foreign key.

1773
02:41:32,622 --> 02:41:38,692
Data Connect then handles this automatically when we define a field with the type of another table.

1774
02:41:39,022 --> 02:41:41,280
For example, author is set to user.

1775
02:41:41,440 --> 02:41:47,076
If you've used Firestore, you would have created few fields like tags as an array of strings.

1776
02:41:48,238 --> 02:41:56,432
While in strict relational databases, you could create a tags table, but dataconnect supports arrays natively in Postgres,

1777
02:41:56,752 --> 02:42:00,196
which is perfect for search filters while keeping the schema simple.

